==English==

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|curve|ball}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|baseball}} A forespin [[pitch]] thrown by rotating the index and middle fingers down and resulting in motion down "curve"
#: {{syn|en|curve<q:slang>|deuce<q:slang>|yakker<q:slang>}}
#: {{ux|en|He bit on a '''curveball''' in the dirt.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} An unexpected turn of events initiated by an opponent or chance.
#: {{syn|en|twist}}
#: {{ux|en|Life has thrown him a few '''curveballs'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2015/03/how-sam-simon-and-the-raven-helped-establish-the-simpsons-artistry/387415/|title=Without Sam Simon, 'The Simpsons' Wouldn't Be What It Is Today|date=2015-03-10|author=David Sims|work=The Atlantic|passage=The season’s first episode, “Bart Gets an F,” got the highest ratings in its history, but “Treehouse of Horror” was the real '''curveball''': a genre-busting effort that proudly punched above the show’s expectations.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2016/12/grammys-albums-of-the-year-beyonce-adele-drake-bieber-sturgill-simpson-2016/509690/|title=The Culture Wars in the Grammy Album Nominations|date=2016-12-06|author=Spencer Kornhaber|work=The Atlantic|passage=There are two '''curveball''' nominations, one perhaps a popularity bid and the other a credibility bid. The popular one is Bieber’s ''Purpose''.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=2021-06-21|medium=podcast|episode=Sam Altman on the A.I. Revolution, Trillionaires and the Future of Political Power|author={{w|Ezra Klein}}|title=The Ezra Klein Show|speaker={{w|Sam Altman}}|time=32:37|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/11/opinion/ezra-klein-podcast-sam-altman.html|passage=And I think that’s an example of where faced with a seemingly intractable political reality, technology produces a solution that is a total '''curveball''' and not that imaginable a few decades ago.}}

